{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-ETWw8DIgrQjZCoMKgTqzREuVWe1jH", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Three-Stage Controlled Rolling and Plate-Mill Microstructure\",\"summary\":\"This community describes controlled rolling of low-alloy steel plate in a plate mill, linking rolling temperature, deformation, recrystallization, grain structure, and mechanical performance. Three-stage controlled rolling proceeds through high-, medium-, and low-temperature stages, with temperature-deformation parameters adjusted to improve strength and low-temperature toughness. The central technical concern is controlling recrystallization and grain growth: unsuitable pass schedules, deformation conditions, or holding conditions can create mixed grain structures that reduce toughness and cannot be removed by later rolling. Subgrains and subgrain hardening provide an additional strengthening pathway, while high-angle boundaries may help arrest brittle cracking.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Three-stage controlled rolling is the community’s central process\",\"explanation\":\"Three-stage controlled rolling is defined as successive processing at high, medium, and low temperatures, with rolling parameters adjusted to obtain higher strength and toughness in low-alloy steel plates. The process is explicitly connected to all three temperature stages and contrasted with ordinary single-stage hot rolling. [Data: Entities (1138, 1168, 1169, 1170); Relationships (1672, 1675, 1692, 1693, 1694)]\\n\\nThe process therefore integrates thermal history and deformation sequencing rather than treating rolling as a single uniform operation. Its importance is reinforced by the relationship between temperature-deformation parameters and lower slab-heating and rolling temperatures. [Data: Entities (1174, 1175, 1176); Relationships (1698, 1699)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Temperature strongly controls deformation effectiveness and austenite evolution\",\"explanation\":\"Deformation effectiveness varies with temperature: it is described as low at high temperatures, higher at medium temperatures, and highest at low temperatures. At high temperature, rapid recrystallization and grain growth reduce retention of deformation effects; at medium temperature, deformation after recrystallization slows austenite grain growth; at low temperature, austenite does not recrystallize between passes and undergoes work hardening. [Data: Entities (1173, 300, 1160, 1161); Relationships (1680, 1670, 1671)]\\n\\nThese relationships make the choice of rolling temperature a key control variable for the resulting grain structure and mechanical properties. The data support a process strategy in which successive stages serve different metallurgical purposes. [Data: Relationships (1692, 1693, 1694)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Plate-mill constraints shape the controlled-rolling route\",\"explanation\":\"The plate mill manufactures plate from slabs but has limited capacity to provide sufficient reduction per pass, making the initial slab-rolling passes particularly difficult in the recrystallization region. Despite this limitation, it allows flexibility in selecting temperature and distributing reduction among recrystallization, non-recrystallization, and two-phase regions. [Data: Entities (361, 894, 895); Relationships (1224, 1244, 1312)]\\n\\nThe associated process route commonly uses roughing passes, a delay or holding period, and finishing passes. The rough-hold-finish schedule is identified with the standard two-stage process, while the three-stage route adds explicit high-, medium-, and low-temperature control. [Data: Entities (929, 935, 936, 956); Relationships (1251, 1259, 1290)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Mixed grain structures are the principal documented quality risk\",\"explanation\":\"Mixed grain structure is a heterogeneous combination of fine and coarse ferrite, upper bainite, polygonal grains, subgrains, and cellular substructures. It can result from deformation in the two-phase region, partial recrystallization during delays, or an improper draught or pass schedule. [Data: Entities (147, 157, 185, 394); Relationships (151, 201, 510, 725, 726)]\\n\\nThe structure is generally undesirable because it is associated with deterioration in low-temperature toughness. Once a mixed gamma-grain structure has formed during rolling, later rolling passes cannot eliminate it, making early temperature, strain, reduction, and holding decisions especially consequential. [Data: Relationships (501, 509, 505, 1527)]\"},{\"summary\":\"High-temperature delays can promote coarse and mixed grain structures\",\"explanation\":\"The first delay in the three-stage process occurs at high temperature and is associated with rapid recrystallization followed by grain growth. The documented result is a coarse-grained structure, and rapid grain growth after recrystallization can induce a mixed gamma-grain structure when deformation conditions are unsuitable. [Data: Entities (941, 947, 393, 396); Relationships (1301, 1262, 508, 507)]\\n\\nAustenite grain growth is also described as occurring between rolling passes, particularly after recrystallization. This establishes holding time and deformation conditions as important controls for preserving a uniform grain structure. [Data: Entities (417, 300); Relationships (546, 1264)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Pass schedule and strain selection are central to grain refinement\",\"explanation\":\"A suitable pass schedule and reduction per pass during the initial passes are associated with obtaining a fine and uniform recrystallized austenite-grain structure. The initial passes are therefore not merely production steps; their reductions determine whether refinement is established before later stages. [Data: Entities (916, 905, 886); Relationships (1239, 1219)]\\n\\nThe data further specify that at least one successive pass should use a strain range larger than that associated with the partial-recrystallization region to obtain uniform, fine-grained austenite and ferrite structures. In the partial-recrystallization region, increasing the number of passes increases the fraction recrystallized. [Data: Entities (1077, 392, 409, 410); Relationships (1527, 534, 535)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Subgrains provide strengthening but have varied crack-arrest capability\",\"explanation\":\"Subgrains form in deformed ferrite when recrystallization is sluggish, particularly during cooling after deformation. Their size and distribution influence mechanical behavior, and subgrain hardening is identified as the principal strengthening mechanism in microstructures containing subgrains, including subgrain-mixed structures. [Data: Entities (264, 266, 278, 777); Relationships (290, 992, 322, 1019)]\\n\\nThe crack-propagation implications depend on boundary structure. Isolated sub-boundaries generally cannot obstruct brittle-crack propagation, whereas clustered subgrains can form high-angle boundaries that arrest brittle cracking. [Data: Entities (811, 812, 813, 831); Relationships (1066, 1068, 1093)]\"}],\"rating\":7.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community has high technical importance because controlled-rolling decisions directly govern steel plate strength, grain refinement, low-temperature toughness, and susceptibility to undesirable microstructures.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790706828, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1564, "prompt_tokens": 7238, "total_tokens": 8802, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 106, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 7235,